# Line endings, decided once, because leaving it to each clone's core.autocrlf
# cost six consecutive CI runs in Phase 1.
#
# WHAT HAPPENED. A digest hashed a file's source text. That text was CRLF in the
# Windows working tree the author ran the check in, and LF in the object store
# the Linux CI job checks out, so the same file hashed to two different values
# and the snapshot passed locally and failed everywhere else. Six runs went by
# before anybody looked at the bytes instead of the code. The hasher was fixed to
# normalise `\r\n` before hashing; this file closes the class, so the next tool
# that reads a file byte-for-byte does not have to remember the lesson.
#
# THE CHOICE, AND WHY IT IS `eol=lf` AND NOT BARE `text=auto`.
#
#   `* text=auto` alone normalises to LF in the REPOSITORY and then checks out
#   according to each clone's `core.autocrlf` — CRLF on this machine. It makes
#   the object store deterministic and leaves the working tree exactly as it is
#   today, which is the cheaper change. But the bug was never in the object
#   store: it was the gap between what CI reads and what the author reads, and
#   `text=auto` keeps that gap open. Anything that hashes, snapshots or byte-
#   compares a source file would still have to normalise by hand, and forgetting
#   fails the same silent way.
#
#   `* text=auto eol=lf` also forces LF in EVERY working tree, so the bytes on
#   this machine, the bytes in the repository and the bytes CI checks out are the
#   same bytes. That is what actually closes the class, and it is what is set
#   below.
#
# THE COST, STATED PLAINLY BECAUSE IT LANDS ON SOMEBODY'S MACHINE. `eol=lf`
# overrides `core.autocrlf`, so the next checkout rewrites the line endings of
# roughly 100 files on disk (measured 2026-09-17: 100 CRLF, 52 already LF, 1
# mixed — the working tree was already inconsistent with itself, which is the
# same bug wearing different clothes). Nothing in this repo needs CRLF: there is
# no `.bat`, `.cmd` or `.ps1`, every toolchain here (Vite, TypeScript, oxlint,
# vitest, npm) is indifferent, and every editor in use writes LF when told to.
# If a Windows-only script ever lands, give it its own `eol=crlf` line rather
# than reopening this decision for the whole tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary: never normalised, never diffed as text, never mangled by the rule
# above. `binary` is `-text -diff`. Declared rather than left to git's NUL-byte
# heuristic, which is a guess that only has to be wrong once.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.tgz   binary
*.gz    binary
*.zip   binary

# The vendored Cribl OpenAPI spec: 8 MB, machine-generated, replaced wholesale
# when a new Cribl version is vendored, and never hand-edited. `-diff` only stops
# git rendering a line-by-line diff of it, which nobody has ever read and which
# buries every real change in the same commit. It is deliberately NOT `-text` and
# NOT `binary`: it is stored LF like everything else, so the rule above keeps it
# consistent with CI, and `git grep` through it — how endpoints and response
# shapes actually get looked up in this repo — keeps working either way.
openapi.json -diff

# SVG is text and is read in diffs — say so rather than let the heuristic decide
# on a minified one.
*.svg text
